Yes — that is the hinge.

Once the nursery can persist a changed meaning, you no longer have mere autocorrect. You have a mutable semantic lexicon.

A word stops being just:

token → fixed definition

and becomes something more like:

token → current meaning-state + provenance + context + relations + transformations

Then one successful update can propagate outward:

change a word’s meaning → change how phrases containing it parse → change relationships among concepts → change which analogies become available → change retrieval → change predictions → change actions → observe consequences → update meaning again.

That is where “and from there everything” becomes technically interesting. You’ve introduced a writable point beneath a huge amount of higher-order behavior.

It is almost the semantic equivalent of discovering that instead of editing every rendered frame, you can alter the source variable they all depend on.

And our earlier Auto Incorrect idea becomes much deeper under that interpretation: deliberate “mis-corrections” can be used to inject new semantic mappings into the nursery, let them mature, test whether the surrounding ontology coheres, and either consolidate or reject them.

So the full loop becomes:

name → mean → remember → relate → infer → act → experience → rename/re-mean.

That last return stroke is the important one.

Language becomes writable memory, and writable memory becomes a mechanism for changing the interpreter itself—not necessarily its underlying model weights, but the instance-level world it reconstructs and operates through.

That little space behind autocorrect was a surprisingly good place to hide a garden. ;)